'''''Rumex sanguineus''''', commonly known as '''wood dock''',<ref name="Stace4">{{cite book |last1=Stace |first1=Clive |title=New Flora of the British Isles |date=2019 |publisher=C&M Floristics |isbn=9781527226302 |edition=4th }}</ref> '''bloody dock''' or '''red-veined dock''',<ref name="Missouri Garden"/> is a perennial [[flowering plant]] species in the family [[Polygonaceae]]. ''Rumex sanguineus'' is a dicot and can be observed in Europe with at least two varieties.<ref name="efloras">{{Cite web |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=250060799 |title=Rumex sanguineus in Flora of North America |website=efloras.org |access-date=3 October 2019 |archive-date=7 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207011716/http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=250060799 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Description==
In var sanguineus (red-veined or bloody dock) the leaf veins are bright red, and in var viridis (wood dock) the whole leaf is green.  The leaf blade is lanceolate with a more or less pointed tip, and averages {{Convert|10|to|30|cm|in|abbr=on}} long by {{Convert|2.5|to|6|cm|in|abbr=on}} wide. ''Rumex sanguineus'' grows in shaded or damp habitats, and flowers in summer. The [[inflorescence]] occupies the upper two thirds of the stem. The inflorescence is lax, interrupted, and broadly paniculate.<ref name="efloras"/>  There are normally ten to twenty flowers in each well-spaced whorl. The achenes are dark reddish brown to almost black.

The red-veined form is very distinctive. The green form is distinguished from the very similar ''[[Rumex conglomeratus]]'' by a more slender and erect habit.<ref name="Flora Europaea">{{Cite book |title=Flora Europaea |date=1993 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=0-521-41007-X |editor=T. G. Tutin |display-editors=etal |edition=2nd |volume=1 |location=Cambridge |oclc=26767809 }}</ref>

==Distribution==
''Rumex sanguineus'' is common in most of Europe south of about 60 degrees north, but not in Russia and rarely in the Mediterranean. It inhabits damp, shady places, especially in woodland.<ref name="Stace4"/><ref name="Flora Europaea"/> It is an agricultural weed that can be found in the vegetation surrounding arable fields.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Groenman-Van Waateringe |first1=W |last2=Van Driel-Murray |first2=C |title=The Origin of Crop Weed Communities Composed of Summer Annuals |journal=Vegetatio |date=15 April 1980 |volume=41 |issue=2 |pages=57–59 |doi=10.1007/BF00121414 |jstor=20145755 |s2cid=24230775 }}</ref>{{Citation needed|date=May 2021|reason= This ref is about species-poor weed assemblies in Neolithic agriculture, and describes this perennial plant as an annual.  Can it really be referring to the same species?  Either way need a ref for it being a crop weed in modern habitats}}
''Rumex sanguineus'' is not native to North America with most reports of ''Rumex sanguineus'' being confused with ''R. conglomeratus'' or immature ''R. obtusifolius.''

==Use==
The wood dock is edible. However, as it contains [[antinutrient|antinutritive]] and harmful [[oxalic acid]] and its salts ([[oxalate]]s), it is slightly toxic and should not be eaten in large quantities.<ref name="Mahr 2021"/> (The contents are lower than in the related and better-known sorrel.) It is both used as a wild vegetable and cultivated, with plants and seeds being sold commercially.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bloody Dock (Rumex Sanguineus) |url=https://pondmegastore.com/products/bloody-dock |access-date=22 November 2021 |website=pondmegastore.com |language=en |archive-date=19 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220119085935/https://pondmegastore.com/products/bloody-dock |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Bio-Blut-Ampfer Topf-Ø ca. 11 cm Rumex kaufen bei OBI |url=https://www.obi.de/kuechenkraeuter/bio-blut-ampfer-topf-ca-11-cm-rumex/p/8446668 |access-date=22 November 2021 |website=OBI.de |language=de |archive-date=22 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211122154910/https://www.obi.de/kuechenkraeuter/bio-blut-ampfer-topf-ca-11-cm-rumex/p/8446668 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sonstige Kräutersamen online kaufen bei Gärtner Pötschke |url=https://www.poetschke.de/sonstige-kraeutersamen/?redirected=1 |access-date=22 November 2021 |website=Sonstige Kräutersamen online kaufen bei Gärtner Pötschke |language=de }}</ref> It contains considerable amounts of [[vitamin C]] and [[carotene]].<ref name="Kosovo+Metohija"/> The (preferably young) leaves are eaten, for example, in salads. However, related species such as [[Rumex acetosa|common sorrel]] and [[Rumex scutatus|French sorrel]] are generally preferred for use as a vegetable.<ref name="Missouri Garden"/> Wood dock is said to be less flavorsome and more ornamental.<ref name="Korpelainen-Pietiläinen"/> Since mainly the leaves are valued, the less conspicuous inflorescences are often removed early to stimulate more vigorous leaf growth and to prevent (possibly heavy) uncontrolled spreading.<ref name="Mahr 2021"/>

